Z||000007||DJRice||08-30-99||01:33 PM||||Oddly enough, my car does the same thing....<p>Hmm.....
Z||000008||Ozzy||08-30-99||05:27 PM||||MEEE TOOOOOOOOO!!!<p>Think we got the rattles licked...whut about the creeeeeeeeking? Almost sounds like the calipers are trying to bend the rotors in two! I just swapped out some shitty ball joints and the creeking is less prominent when the car is not moving but when it is moving the noise is just as bad if not worse. I'm yet to have someone try thebrakes with the front end in the air while I listen to where the noise is originating from.<p>Ozzy
Z||000009||K9||09-01-99||10:30 PM||||I have the same problem with my 85 GT. I have inspected every corner of my front end and have not found the cause.It began only when I changed the brakes for the first time so I am going to change the brakes again to see if this helps.
Z||000010||GWMORGAN||09-01-99||11:45 PM||||On the front break pads are tension braces which allow them to fit snugly in the calipers.Over time they weaken and just float inside the calipers which cause a rattling when the break is not being applied. To correct the situation; remove the pads, brace the tensioners outward and snap them back inside the calipers.
Z||000011||Pretender||09-02-99||02:20 AM||||mine too!, i even upgraded to the crossdrilled rotors and carbo-met. pads, still the noise goes on. i gave up on it, it isn't the the springs on the pads because there pretty "springy".,  It does sound like the piston is pushing too hard or something. what ever it is it's been there ever since i've had it(5 years). 
Z||000012||Speadraser||09-02-99||02:53 AM||||<br>
Z||000013||Speadraser||09-02-99||03:06 AM||||Hello all,<br>I think I may have the solution to the sqealing or creeking WHILE the brakes are applied problem.  The problem lies usually with the pads themselves.  There is a right angle at the leading edge of the pad that contacts the rotor.  If you remove both pads and take them to a bench grinder and bevel off the 90 deg of the leading edge of the material to say a 45 deg angle this should eliminate the problem.  I'm talking about just 3/8" from the edge.  Be sure to remove any grease from the pads and rotor before road testing.  I learned this trick a few years ago and have never had brake sqeak on any of the cars I've owned(organic or semi-metallic, never tried carbon-metallic).  You can also apply the treatment to the trailing edges to eliminate any sqeaking in reverse braking.  I always do both edges anyway.  Be sure to take the cut down to the approximate depth of the rivets or the sqeaking may return further down the line.  Hope this helps.<br>Speadraser<br>Speadraser@hotmail.com
Z||000014||batboy||09-02-99||04:24 AM||||The 85 GT I just bought creaks a little when you apply the brakes. Sounds like to me it's coming from where the brake petal pivets on the petal assembly. I'll let you know sometime this weekend if I've found a place down there to lubricate and whether it fixes my problem.
